Leaf Thumb function with a conditional loop branch and NO push {lr}: target starts `adds r3,r0,#0; movs r1,#0; ldr r2,=0x03001150; b ...` and ends `adds r0,r1,#0; bx lr`. The only available compiler (agbcc) emits `push {lr}` + `pop {pc}` (with -O2) or `push {lr}` + `pop {r1}; bx r1` (default -mthumb-interwork) for any function containing a branch, even a leaf with no calls; old_agbcc is not available on this project. This is the known old_agbcc-only class (see project knowledge). No C shape avoids the push. Body logic: search D_03001150 (s32 array, stride 2 words = 8 bytes) for first element == arg0 among indices 0..0x13; return index (0x14 if not found):
extern s32 D_03001150[];
s32 sub_080A44DC(s32 arg0){ s32 i; for(i=0;i<=0x13;i++){ if(D_03001150[i*2]==arg0) break; } return i; }
Under agbcc -O2 the body matches instruction-for-instruction (score 840) except the push/pop lr. Set aside unless an old_agbcc compiler is added.

Only 2 regalloc differences remain under agbcc -O2 (25/25 insns identical, 99.6%): target `ldr r1,[sp]; strb r5,[r1]` vs ours `ldr r0,[sp]; strb r5,[r0]`. It cannot get closer by C-shape: the call is fixed 3-arg `sub_080A0BE4(&s, arg1, arg2)` (arg1/arg2 forwarded, which is what pins r1/r2 and makes r4=sp for the 0xC STRH), and every formulation of the final `dst[0]=0` (through the struct field, a temp, a S* pointer, s32/void fields, no-prototype callee, -O3, reordered stores) gives r0 instead of r1. This is a pure register-allocation tie-break in agbcc; leave the exact source for the next agent:
